{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key figure in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by instability as he inherited the county while still a minor. His mother served as regent until he came of age. Raymond was raised in the court of Jerusalem and became well connected to the nobility of the Levant. His upbringing prepared him for the complex politics of the Crusader kingdoms. He was known for his intelligence and diplomatic skills which served him well during his long tenure as count.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the defense and diplomacy of the Crusader states. He was a close ally of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent of Jerusalem in 1174 and again in 1183 during Baldwin’s declining health. His regency was controversial because of his cautious approach to military action. He often preferred negotiation over confrontation especially with Saladin. This cautious stance drew criticism from more aggressive nobles such as Guy of Lusignan and Reynald of Châtillon. Raymond’s diplomacy was seen as weak by some but his actions helped prevent immediate collapse of the Crusader territories during a time of great vulnerability.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III’s legacy is tied to his role in the lead up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who tried to prevent the disastrous campaign that led to the defeat of the Crusader army. He warned against attacking Saladin’s forces without proper preparation. His advice was ignored and the Crusader army was annihilated at Hattin. Raymond himself survived the battle and fled to Tripoli. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of the city. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy in the Crusader states. His son Raymond IV succeeded him and continued to defend Tripoli against Muslim advances. Raymond III is remembered as a thoughtful and prudent leader who struggled to maintain stability in a time of rising chaos and internal division. His life reflects the difficult choices faced by Crusader nobles caught between survival and honor.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2927336096763611}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5882569551467896}, {"token": " key", "logprob":